Z||000000||jeffndebrus||10-16-2001||05:07 PM||||can all you friendly fiero owners out there give me a list of which cars I might find a 3800sc II in ? Year, model, and make would be great.  It seems as if everyone has an oppinion about which engine to swap into the fiero but after reading all your comments- the 3800sc is looking good to me because of performance and fuel efficiency--comments welcome-- also) is the 3.4 from camaro the same as the 3.4TDC that you talk about?<BR>Thank you and enjoy those fiero cars!!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000001||Jay||10-16-2001||05:39 PM||||I'm not sure what year the series 2 came out but you can find them in GTP's/Bonnevilles.  If you go with a newer engine that is OBD2 you may want to wire it up to a OBD1 ECM, make life alot easier.<P>------------------<BR>Jay<BR>1986 Northstar GT<BR>www.members.home.net/jaygrande<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/cayuga3_3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||jeffndebrus||10-16-2001||07:38 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Jay:<BR><B>I'm not sure what year the series 2 came out but you can find them in GTP's/Bonnevilles.  If you go with a newer engine that is OBD2 you may want to wire it up to a OBD1 ECM, make life alot easier.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE>Thank you Jay--I still have that 4.0 aurora engine available to me however it is just the crated engine- no ecm computer no wiring harness ect. I think I may be better off going a different route?<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Tryxalon||10-16-2001||08:01 PM||||1995 and newer <P>Olds 88/98/LS/LSS <BR>Pontiac Bonnevilles<BR>Buick LeSabre/Electra <P>All have the 3800 Series II as the standard engine. (not all have the Supercharged, but all are 3800) That's about 12 million possibilities right there. <P>It was also an option on <BR>Buick Regal<BR>Chevy Lumina (some) <BR>Olds Cutlass Supreme <BR>Pontiac Grand Prix<BR>to give another several million possibilities.  <P><BR>Probably the most common, and perhaps the "best" GM engine right now (in some people's opinion). The non-supercharged 3.8L (prior to 91)  and 3800 Series I (prior to 95) have several examples that have reached 300,000 or more miles on them. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||terryk||10-16-2001||09:23 PM||||96 and newer. 95's are Series I.<P>Also, 96 is the only year withthe 4T60E. The 4T65E came in 97.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Jay||10-16-2001||10:13 PM||||The Aurora engine is a beauty.  I'd go with that just to be different.  It's basically a Northstar swap.  Now that I know how to get the ECM to scan (by using the Instrument cluster)it would be a breeze!||||reg||1
Z||000006||jeffndebrus||10-16-2001||10:51 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Jay:<BR><B>The Aurora engine is a beauty.  I'd go with that just to be different.  It's basically a Northstar swap.  Now that I know how to get the ECM to scan (by using the Instrument cluster)it would be a breeze!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I wish I had your confidence-- I would really love to have that aurora engine in my 85notchback just to be different-- isn't that why we like fieros to begin with.  I wouldn't know where to start though. any suggestions?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||BN Boomer||10-17-2001||05:25 AM||||What are the differences between the Series I and Series II 3800s? Or the 4T60E and the 4T65E transmissions?||||reg||1
Z||000008||David DeVoe||10-17-2001||10:48 AM||||I work in the plant that builds the 3800 Series II and was part of the design team that did the initial workup on it. We started building them in July of 95. The supercharged version is used on All Park Avenue Ultra's, SSEI Bonnevilles, and is available on Regal GS, Pontiac GTP. The engine is in my opinion one of the most reliable that GM has ever built. We put them in taxis (non supercharged) in LA and after 200k miles took them apart and measured every moving part. Most showed no wear at all or were still in spec. <BR>If I ever swap an engine into my 86SE you can bet it'll be a Series II.<BR>Dave||||reg||1
Z||000009||terryk||10-17-2001||12:10 PM||||"We started building them in July of 95. "<P>...for the 96 model year.||||reg||1
Z||000010||VISCERAL||10-17-2001||01:41 PM||||Hey Davis Devoe, what's the hp and torque settings of a sc3800 if one put one on the back of a fiero w/5 spd. I would like to know what is the real rear wheel hp and torque. I've heard that the motors are over rated in the dyno dept. Sc3800 actually gets 180hp at the rear wheels? Whats the torque?||||reg||1
Z||000011||terryk||10-17-2001||11:00 PM||||I don't know, the dyno results I've seen put them right at spec. 240HP and 280 torque. dyno tests around 210HP and 235 torque at the wheels on the automatic.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Master_Sushi||10-18-2001||09:54 AM||||I remember hearing from somewhere that the 3800 series II was rated one of the top 10 engines in the world. I can't remember where though  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">. It really is a nice engine!<P>Also, the 3.4 in the camaros was a OHV I think. The DOHC 3.4 was only in front wheel drive cars, like luminas. The 3.4 twin cam is a SCREAMER of an engine. I'd take a 3.4dohc over a 3.8pushrod anyday in my fiero. I've driven a few 3.8NA's(5 speed and auto) and they really arn't "that" impressive. They seem to have no high RPM power. I have yet to drive a supercharged one though.||||reg||1
